            <ArticleTitle>Early factors leading to later obesity: interactions of the microbiome, epigenome, and nutrition.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>134-42</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cppeds.2015.03.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1538-5442(15)00030-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity is a major public health problem in the United States and many other countries. Childhood obesity rates have risen extensively over the last several decades with the numbers continuing to rise. Obese and overweight children are at high risk of becoming overweight adolescents and adults. The causes are multifactorial and are affected by various genetic, behavioral, and environmental factors. This review aims to discuss a previously under-recognized antecedent of obesity and related chronic metabolic diseases such as heart disease and diabetes. Specifically, we highlight the relationship of the microbial ecology of the gastrointestinal tract during early development and the consequent effects on metabolism, epigenetics, and inflammatory responses that can subsequently result in metabolic syndrome. Although studies in this area are just beginning, this area of research is rapidly expanding and may lead to early life interventions that may have significant impacts in the prevention of obesity.</AbstractText>
